#let abstract-content = [
Exergames and automation for rehabilitation have shown to be effective in providing personalized therapy to patients with various conditions. However, therapists' sense of agency in relation to AI-supported computational systems for exergame-based rehabilitation remains largely under-explored, especially across all four main phases of therapy. With the aim of discovering this gap, the study investigates therapists sense of agency through a participatory Research through Design (RtD) approach by employing two main methods: (1) a contextual inquiry conducted through a case study (Brain Stimulation) including interviews and observation, and (2) individual participatory speculative co-design sessions. Eight participants in total, with an average of *X years* were involved in the study: five participated in the contextual inquiry and five in the co-design session. The results from the affinity diagramming indicate that under certain conditions, therapists' maintain a sense of agency in AI supported computational systems for exergame-based, provided that their responsibility and care over their patient is not threatened. Although, these conditions change across the four main phases of therapy. Collectively, this study contributes to more-than-human design through the lens of therapists agency within exergame-based rehabilitation.
]

